  "hints": [
    "If a subsequence is unique to one input string, what happens when you extend it to that whole string?",
    "Only whole input strings need to be candidates. Reject a candidate if any other input position contains it as a subsequence.",
    "For each candidate i, scan every j!=i with a subsequence pointer; maximize the lengths of candidates that all other strings reject.",
    "Do not discard equal strings before checking: two equal occurrences disqualify one another. Subsequence matching skips characters but never reorders them."
  ],
  "editorial": "Suppose a subsequence is unique to string s. If s itself occurred as a subsequence of another input, that other input would also contain the shorter candidate, a contradiction. Thus some longest answer is an entire input string. Compare every candidate against every other position using an order-preserving pointer. With m strings and maximum length L this costs O(m^2*L) time and O(1) extra space. A separate oracle enumerates all subsequences and counts containing input positions; it does not rely on the whole-string-candidate theorem.",
